This work builds directly on published research and open source by others.

BeeKEM — Ink & Switch (https://www.inkandswitch.com/)
  The concurrent-TreeKEM design this engine implements, and below the PcsKey
  boundary a faithful port of their Rust `beekem` crate. Our port is validated
  against that implementation by a differential oracle: on shared scenarios the
  two must agree byte for byte. BeeKEM solves the problem that makes this
  protocol possible at all — keeping a group's keys in agreement when several
  members change the group concurrently, with no server ordering their changes.
  Any errors in what we built on top are ours, not theirs.

DCGKA — Weidner, Kleppmann, Hugenroth, Beresford (CCS 2021)
  "Key Agreement for Decentralized Secure Group Messaging with Strong Security
  Guarantees." The proof obligations our Dynamic Group Management must satisfy —
  determinism, add-only entry, no-remove-undo, convergence — are theirs.

p2panda-auth (https://p2panda.org/)
  The strong-remove resolver semantics our DGM implements: a removal takes
  effect even when it races concurrent operations, so no member can be
  "un-removed" by a concurrent update.

Cremers et al.
  Signing-key rotation for post-compromise security of authenticity, which this
  layer applies to per-message protocol signing keys.
